%I A401270 #18 Oct 21 2026 18:07:57
%S A401270 10,13,82,100,119
%N A401270 Numbers k such that 10^(2*k+4) + 777*10^(k+1) + 1 is prime.
%C A401270 The prime has the palindromic decimal form 1, followed by k zeros, then 777, then k zeros, then 1.
%C A401270 Its digit sum is always 23, so it is never divisible by 3.
%C A401270 If k is congruent to 0 or 2 modulo 6, the number is divisible by 13, so no such k occurs.
%C A401270 The k = 13 term, 1000000000000077700000000000001 (31 digits), was noted as prime in Atlas Obscura (2016, updated 2022); it is the 777 analog of Belphegor's prime.
%C A401270 Searched to k = 1250 by the author; BPSW finds no further terms up to k = 5000 (_Alper Ferudun_).
%H A401270 Eric Grundhauser, <a href="https://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/belphegors-prime-number-superstitions">How a Mathematician Turned an Obscure Number Into a Scary Story</a>, Atlas Obscura, 2016.
%H A401270 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belphegor&#39;s_prime">Belphegor's prime</a>.
%e A401270 k = 0 gives 17771 = 13 * 1367, which is not prime.
%e A401270 k = 10 gives 1000000000077700000000001, which is prime.
%e A401270 k = 13 gives 1000000000000077700000000000001, which is prime.
%o A401270 (Python)
%o A401270 from gmpy2 import is_prime
%o A401270 def ok(k):
%o A401270     return is_prime(10**(2*k+4) + 777*10**(k+1) + 1, 25)
%o A401270 print([k for k in range(200) if ok(k)])
%Y A401270 Cf. A232448 (the 666-center analog), A002385.
%K A401270 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A401270 1,1
%A A401270 _Anthony Bean_, Oct 19 2026
